# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout by default, and for a shell
# script that is not a cosmetic difference: a build.sh with carriage returns
# fails on Linux with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M", and install.sh - which
# people run by piping it straight from a URL into sh - fails the same way.
# So the text files here are pinned to LF regardless of the machine they are
# checked out on.

# Everything text, normalised in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Scripts that must have LF wherever they run.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.go    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf

# Windows scripts want CRLF. cmd.exe tolerates LF in most cases and then
# surprises you on a line continuation, and PowerShell here-strings are worse;
# both are only ever run on Windows, so there is nothing to gain by pinning
# them the other way.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# The embedded Swagger UI assets are third-party and vendored verbatim. They
# are marked as such so they do not clutter a diff, and are left byte-for-byte
# as published rather than being normalised.
static/swagger/** binary linguist-vendored

# Captured API responses, used as test fixtures. Normalising them would be
# rewriting the evidence.
testdata/** -text
